WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:00.879
- Life is corked.

00:00:00.879 --> 00:00:02.827
I'm sure all of you have
your lives planned out,

00:00:02.827 --> 00:00:04.688
and the one thing I can
absolutely guarantee

00:00:04.688 --> 00:00:06.001
is it won't work out that way.

00:00:06.001 --> 00:00:09.009
I accept the world as
it is, and I go forward.

00:00:09.009 --> 00:00:11.191
If you don't have passion for your work

00:00:11.191 --> 00:00:12.842
or the drive to make a better life

00:00:12.842 --> 00:00:14.123
for your kids and your own,

00:00:14.123 --> 00:00:17.052
then you won't have what
it takes to keep you going.

00:00:17.052 --> 00:00:21.604
You're going to have to be
flexible and nimble in your life.

00:00:21.604 --> 00:00:23.027
You don't buy a thick skin,

00:00:23.027 --> 00:00:24.311
and you don't even know you have it

00:00:24.311 --> 00:00:26.752
until somebody starts
to try to puncture it.

00:00:26.752 --> 00:00:28.483
Most of the books I've read didn't apply

00:00:28.483 --> 00:00:30.423
directly to my job or industry

00:00:30.423 --> 00:00:33.634
but I've applied their
lessons in unexpected ways.

00:00:33.634 --> 00:00:37.081
You also have to peak at the
time it's an Olympic year.

00:00:37.081 --> 00:00:39.115
Yeah, you could be the
fastest runner in the world,

00:00:39.115 --> 00:00:40.230
and it's an off year.

00:00:40.230 --> 00:00:42.289
Being successful in business

00:00:42.289 --> 00:00:45.957
means part of it is try to
guess what's going to happen,

00:00:45.957 --> 00:00:47.735
part of it is trying to guess the future.

00:00:47.735 --> 00:00:51.233
I came home and told my wife
I was quitting and she cried.

00:00:51.233 --> 00:00:53.083
The people we get,

00:00:53.083 --> 00:00:55.279
a kind of person at Goldman Sachs,

00:00:55.279 --> 00:00:58.973
who really wants to be
an influential person.

00:00:58.973 --> 00:01:00.705
- He's an American business executive.

00:01:00.705 --> 00:01:03.310
He's a CEO and chairman of Goldman Sachs.

00:01:03.310 --> 00:01:06.071
He's one of the highest paid
executives on Wall Street.

00:01:06.071 --> 00:01:07.431
He's Lloyd Blankfein,

00:01:07.431 --> 00:01:09.662
and here are his top 10 rules for success.

00:01:09.662 --> 00:01:10.708
(air whooshing)

00:01:10.708 --> 00:01:12.821
(heavy object thuds)

00:01:12.821 --> 00:01:13.654
- Life is corked.

00:01:13.654 --> 00:01:15.592
I'm sure all of you have
your lives planned out,

00:01:15.592 --> 00:01:17.264
and the one thing I can
absolutely guarantee

00:01:17.264 --> 00:01:19.250
is it won't work out that way

00:01:19.250 --> 00:01:20.905
'cause just things happen.

00:01:20.905 --> 00:01:23.552
First of all, the world
changes in the world of Oz

00:01:23.552 --> 00:01:24.872
so whatever plans you're making,

00:01:24.872 --> 00:01:27.308
it's based on a static
world that won't be there

00:01:27.308 --> 00:01:30.899
because things will
happen, and you'll change,

00:01:30.899 --> 00:01:32.050
or maybe you won't even change,

00:01:32.050 --> 00:01:34.321
maybe you just
misidentifying yourself now.

00:01:34.321 --> 00:01:36.592
We don't know, which is a
perfectly appropriate way to be

00:01:36.592 --> 00:01:39.071
when you're young or in my age

00:01:39.071 --> 00:01:40.872
'cause who knows?

00:01:40.872 --> 00:01:43.134
- What would you change about the way

00:01:43.134 --> 00:01:46.968
that you ended up as the
CEO of Goldman Sachs?

00:01:46.968 --> 00:01:48.241
- You know, I don't have that.

00:01:48.241 --> 00:01:50.420
You know how the mayor just said

00:01:50.420 --> 00:01:52.394
he looks forward and the next
day is going to be better?

00:01:52.394 --> 00:01:55.227
And I think a kar-ler-i of that is

00:01:57.283 --> 00:02:01.002
I accept the world as
it is, and I go forward.

00:02:01.002 --> 00:02:04.810
I mean I could do an archeological dig

00:02:04.810 --> 00:02:06.860
but I can't go back and change it

00:02:06.860 --> 00:02:09.262
so I don't spend a lot
of time fussing about it.

00:02:09.262 --> 00:02:10.410
I want to learn from it

00:02:10.410 --> 00:02:12.101
but I don't want to wring
my hands and incriminate

00:02:12.101 --> 00:02:14.335
so it's never, "I wish I were taller,

00:02:14.335 --> 00:02:16.821
"I wish I could run faster."

00:02:16.821 --> 00:02:18.217
- Wish you had a lower handicap?

00:02:18.217 --> 00:02:19.268
- Wish I had a lower handicap.
(laughing)

00:02:19.268 --> 00:02:20.511
I have plenty of handicap

00:02:20.511 --> 00:02:23.740
but not the low one in golf that I want.

00:02:23.740 --> 00:02:27.070
But I don't spend a lot of
time thinking about that

00:02:27.070 --> 00:02:29.057
but if you put me to it,

00:02:29.057 --> 00:02:30.296
I'd tell you another thing

00:02:30.296 --> 00:02:32.645
that anybody who had a
chance to revisit the past

00:02:32.645 --> 00:02:34.538
with what he knows now

00:02:34.538 --> 00:02:36.190
would be crazy not to do it

00:02:36.190 --> 00:02:37.680
but it's just not available

00:02:37.680 --> 00:02:39.447
so how much time are you
going to spend on it?

00:02:39.447 --> 00:02:41.885
In my career, I've been
fortunate to know and work

00:02:41.885 --> 00:02:45.613
with many of this country's
top CEOs and business leaders.

00:02:45.613 --> 00:02:49.472
I'm always struck by a certain
passion that defines them.

00:02:49.472 --> 00:02:52.318
While they may be wealthy and powerful,

00:02:52.318 --> 00:02:55.206
their passion goes beyond money and power.

00:02:55.206 --> 00:02:59.052
I won't stand here and tell
you that those are bad things.

00:02:59.052 --> 00:03:00.135
They're pretty good

00:03:00.135 --> 00:03:03.279
but only if you have a
larger purpose in mind.

00:03:03.279 --> 00:03:05.757
If you don't have passion for your work

00:03:05.757 --> 00:03:07.447
or the drive to make a better life

00:03:07.447 --> 00:03:08.931
for your kids and your own,

00:03:08.931 --> 00:03:11.975
then you won't have what
it takes to keep you going.

00:03:11.975 --> 00:03:16.052
You're going to have to be
flexible and nimble in your life.

00:03:16.052 --> 00:03:19.906
That's the characteristic you want to get.

00:03:19.906 --> 00:03:22.435
It's not important whether
you know what the product is,

00:03:22.435 --> 00:03:25.610
or sure, or what's hot and what's not,

00:03:25.610 --> 00:03:28.470
or how to execute this particular thing.

00:03:28.470 --> 00:03:30.041
You need to learn business,

00:03:30.041 --> 00:03:34.687
and you need to learn how to
question and have an open mind.

00:03:34.687 --> 00:03:38.899
You need to know the process
of thinking about things.

00:03:38.899 --> 00:03:40.301
You notice how people can go around

00:03:40.301 --> 00:03:41.868
and be an investor in the industry,

00:03:41.868 --> 00:03:43.272
turn around and without doing anything,

00:03:43.272 --> 00:03:45.389
be a great investor in another industry,

00:03:45.389 --> 00:03:49.025
or some CEO who one day is
running Ford Motor Company,

00:03:49.025 --> 00:03:53.582
and then can run Boeing
or different industries?

00:03:53.582 --> 00:03:55.132
The most important thing

00:03:55.132 --> 00:03:57.904
is not the particular characteristics

00:03:57.904 --> 00:03:59.887
of a particular industry or thing,

00:03:59.887 --> 00:04:02.381
it's how to be a good CEO

00:04:02.381 --> 00:04:05.365
just like it's how to
have presentation skills,

00:04:05.365 --> 00:04:06.481
how to be a good manager,

00:04:06.481 --> 00:04:07.633
how to be a good subordinate,

00:04:07.633 --> 00:04:09.287
how to be a good partner,

00:04:09.287 --> 00:04:13.542
how to use people, how
to be usable by people.

00:04:13.542 --> 00:04:17.030
And those are the things
that you have to learn.

00:04:17.030 --> 00:04:18.562
Now you can only learn it.

00:04:18.562 --> 00:04:20.250
You can't learn it abstractly,

00:04:20.250 --> 00:04:22.377
and you can't really learn it in school.

00:04:22.377 --> 00:04:23.621
You have to learn it by doing

00:04:23.621 --> 00:04:26.810
but you have to take those
lessons and extrapolate it.

00:04:26.810 --> 00:04:30.143
So the things that I did in the markets.

00:04:32.532 --> 00:04:35.819
And the context of even in
investment banking and advisory,

00:04:35.819 --> 00:04:38.216
those are entirely different things

00:04:38.216 --> 00:04:39.912
30 years ago than they are today.

00:04:39.912 --> 00:04:43.758
They're different five years
ago than they are today.

00:04:43.758 --> 00:04:46.709
We talk about time of great change.

00:04:46.709 --> 00:04:47.542
Okay.

00:04:47.542 --> 00:04:49.939
Every time is a time of great change.

00:04:49.939 --> 00:04:51.476
And I will tell you,

00:04:51.476 --> 00:04:54.501
and the technology is driving it.

00:04:54.501 --> 00:04:55.590
And you don't know.

00:04:55.590 --> 00:04:57.527
Everybody always
remembers getting it right

00:04:57.527 --> 00:04:58.360
when they thought forward

00:04:58.360 --> 00:05:00.061
because you always acquire it,

00:05:00.061 --> 00:05:02.249
after acquired information

00:05:02.249 --> 00:05:03.569
always affects your views.

00:05:03.569 --> 00:05:05.670
You don't remember what you used to think.

00:05:05.670 --> 00:05:07.487
And so that's just the nature of things.

00:05:07.487 --> 00:05:11.084
And the people who'd be
successful in their lives

00:05:11.084 --> 00:05:13.643
are the people who are the most flexible,

00:05:13.643 --> 00:05:15.012
who can absorb the most,

00:05:15.012 --> 00:05:18.429
who are open to learn, who are coachable,

00:05:19.271 --> 00:05:22.975
who are not too proud
to dig in to new things.

00:05:22.975 --> 00:05:24.277
Who was an entrepreneur

00:05:24.277 --> 00:05:26.012
where everything is a smooth ride,

00:05:26.012 --> 00:05:27.418
and you go through these things,

00:05:27.418 --> 00:05:28.782
and I tell you and you get criticized

00:05:28.782 --> 00:05:29.864
and you have to have a thick skin.

00:05:29.864 --> 00:05:30.990
Yeah.

00:05:30.990 --> 00:05:32.001
(laughing)

00:05:32.001 --> 00:05:32.834
- [Interviewer] You've been there?

00:05:32.834 --> 00:05:33.667
- I've been there.

00:05:33.667 --> 00:05:34.500
(laughing)

00:05:34.500 --> 00:05:37.481
And by the way, you
don't buy a thick skin,

00:05:37.481 --> 00:05:38.809
and you don't even know you have it

00:05:38.809 --> 00:05:41.375
until somebody starts
to try to puncture it,

00:05:41.375 --> 00:05:42.981
and then you discover what you're made of.

00:05:42.981 --> 00:05:45.756
Be a well-rounded, complete person.

00:05:45.756 --> 00:05:49.787
Many of you will go for further
educational career training.

00:05:49.787 --> 00:05:51.601
Of course, it's important to learn

00:05:51.601 --> 00:05:54.154
the things that you need to make a living

00:05:54.154 --> 00:05:56.838
but don't forget to read
and to learn history,

00:05:56.838 --> 00:05:59.676
literature, and about current events.

00:05:59.676 --> 00:06:01.862
You'll be more interesting to others,

00:06:01.862 --> 00:06:03.634
more interesting to yourself,

00:06:03.634 --> 00:06:06.734
and you'll be more successful in your job.

00:06:06.734 --> 00:06:08.471
Most of the books I've read didn't apply

00:06:08.471 --> 00:06:10.861
directly to my job or industry

00:06:10.861 --> 00:06:13.991
but I've applied their
lessons in unexpected ways.

00:06:13.991 --> 00:06:16.102
If you're an Olympic athlete or something

00:06:16.102 --> 00:06:17.339
like you get to the high,

00:06:17.339 --> 00:06:21.311
you also have to peak at the
time it's an Olympic year.

00:06:21.311 --> 00:06:22.836
Yeah, you could be the
fastest runner in the world,

00:06:22.836 --> 00:06:24.488
and it's an off year,

00:06:24.488 --> 00:06:26.218
and so by the time you get to the Olympics

00:06:26.218 --> 00:06:28.624
three years later, you aren't anymore

00:06:28.624 --> 00:06:31.799
so there's a lot of luck
involved in things in the world.

00:06:31.799 --> 00:06:34.279
I never lived my life
thinking about my next job

00:06:34.279 --> 00:06:36.555
because I was always in
the last job I could have

00:06:36.555 --> 00:06:38.660
as far as I could tell,

00:06:38.660 --> 00:06:41.510
and I think it's a huge advantage for you

00:06:41.510 --> 00:06:45.226
to live in the moment, and
not to be seething too much.

00:06:45.226 --> 00:06:46.796
I mean obviously you plan

00:06:46.796 --> 00:06:49.392
even though your plans never work out

00:06:49.392 --> 00:06:53.559
but the secret is to do a great
job with what you're doing.

00:06:54.677 --> 00:06:56.369
You know, what you should do

00:06:56.369 --> 00:06:59.272
is you should perform as if
you were in your next job

00:06:59.272 --> 00:07:02.674
but not seethe with the
ambition to get there,

00:07:02.674 --> 00:07:03.507
and project that.

00:07:03.507 --> 00:07:05.122
You make people nervous as hell

00:07:05.122 --> 00:07:06.955
and you kill yourself.

00:07:08.357 --> 00:07:11.684
When I was in high school, I went to,

00:07:11.684 --> 00:07:14.489
because you heard all those
advisory boards, I was at,

00:07:14.489 --> 00:07:17.149
went to Harvard College,
Harvard Law School,

00:07:17.149 --> 00:07:19.248
and when I went to the college

00:07:19.248 --> 00:07:22.386
from the high school I came from,

00:07:22.386 --> 00:07:23.487
it never dawned on me

00:07:23.487 --> 00:07:26.285
that I'd get into that
kind of an Ivy League.

00:07:26.285 --> 00:07:29.600
It just didn't happen but I applied,

00:07:29.600 --> 00:07:31.961
and I never thought about
it after really I applied

00:07:31.961 --> 00:07:34.932
'cause it wasn't on my scope,

00:07:34.932 --> 00:07:37.633
and so when I got a letter,

00:07:37.633 --> 00:07:40.016
I didn't have any anxiety to it,

00:07:40.016 --> 00:07:41.212
and when I got that letter,

00:07:41.212 --> 00:07:43.439
I just was like ecstatic and like this,

00:07:43.439 --> 00:07:45.803
and then my mind opened up.

00:07:45.803 --> 00:07:48.703
Even the process of that was great.

00:07:48.703 --> 00:07:50.536
When I was in college,

00:07:51.549 --> 00:07:53.990
and I applied to law school,

00:07:53.990 --> 00:07:55.813
I went down to the mailbox every day.

00:07:55.813 --> 00:07:57.215
I knew the consequences of it.

00:07:57.215 --> 00:07:59.321
I was with a bunch of
people who also wanted it,

00:07:59.321 --> 00:08:02.962
were vying for it, and I was
like torturing myself over,

00:08:02.962 --> 00:08:04.115
and I was imagining what if I do get in,

00:08:04.115 --> 00:08:05.113
what if I don't get in,

00:08:05.113 --> 00:08:06.924
what are the consequence
of getting in or not?

00:08:06.924 --> 00:08:08.091
And I tell you

00:08:09.507 --> 00:08:12.507
it affected my life, and it affected

00:08:13.387 --> 00:08:15.091
the functionality.

00:08:15.091 --> 00:08:15.924
That's a difference.

00:08:15.924 --> 00:08:19.588
I tell you if you could
approach every opportunity

00:08:19.588 --> 00:08:21.774
like I did the college one,

00:08:21.774 --> 00:08:24.746
you'll be better and you'll be happier,

00:08:24.746 --> 00:08:27.141
and if you live the life tormented

00:08:27.141 --> 00:08:30.280
about what are my
prospects for the next one,

00:08:30.280 --> 00:08:32.168
when life is random,

00:08:32.168 --> 00:08:34.547
and there's some chance
involved in things,

00:08:34.547 --> 00:08:35.664
you're going to be tortured.

00:08:35.664 --> 00:08:37.687
Being successful in business

00:08:37.687 --> 00:08:41.358
means part of it is try to
guess what's going to happen,

00:08:41.358 --> 00:08:43.710
part of it is trying to guess the future.

00:08:43.710 --> 00:08:46.319
History is as important as economics.

00:08:46.319 --> 00:08:47.561
- Okay.

00:08:47.561 --> 00:08:48.394
- Right?

00:08:48.394 --> 00:08:50.284
In other words, being able to see,

00:08:50.284 --> 00:08:52.639
"Gee, this period of time,

00:08:52.639 --> 00:08:56.568
"it's not the same as the
1980s but it's like it."

00:08:56.568 --> 00:08:59.699
I remember another time
when politics were poisoned,

00:08:59.699 --> 00:09:02.299
or this or that, or this
situation in the world today,

00:09:02.299 --> 00:09:06.469
or the conflict we're
seeing in the Middle East,

00:09:06.469 --> 00:09:09.056
how was that in the
temperament of the times,

00:09:09.056 --> 00:09:10.900
and the reaction it produces

00:09:10.900 --> 00:09:12.356
compared to the period of time

00:09:12.356 --> 00:09:16.448
when everybody was worried
about the rise of Russia,

00:09:16.448 --> 00:09:18.721
or the rise of, what's that like?

00:09:18.721 --> 00:09:20.601
In other words, nothing is new.

00:09:20.601 --> 00:09:23.351
Everything of course is different

00:09:24.365 --> 00:09:26.198
but the cycles repeat,

00:09:27.111 --> 00:09:28.594
and I think the study of history

00:09:28.594 --> 00:09:29.956
gives you a feeling,

00:09:29.956 --> 00:09:31.567
knowing that nothing stays the same

00:09:31.567 --> 00:09:33.998
and everything is evolved,
nothing is static,

00:09:33.998 --> 00:09:36.308
that's an important lesson to learn.

00:09:36.308 --> 00:09:37.716
If I want to be good in economics,

00:09:37.716 --> 00:09:39.148
I'd want to know economic history.

00:09:39.148 --> 00:09:41.128
If I want to be a good scientist,

00:09:41.128 --> 00:09:43.101
I want to know the history of science,

00:09:43.101 --> 00:09:45.501
and that would maybe help me understand

00:09:45.501 --> 00:09:47.834
the receptivity of the world

00:09:48.846 --> 00:09:51.951
to advances today and why
some things get accepted

00:09:51.951 --> 00:09:54.318
and why some things don't.

00:09:54.318 --> 00:09:55.151
Yeah.

00:09:55.151 --> 00:09:56.234
I thought so.

00:09:58.123 --> 00:10:01.210
Anyway that's just my
prejudice and my background

00:10:01.210 --> 00:10:04.509
has made me appreciate
knowing the history of things

00:10:04.509 --> 00:10:06.333
and not just the science of things.

00:10:06.333 --> 00:10:09.600
After college, I went to
law school for three years,

00:10:09.600 --> 00:10:11.083
and then into my dream job

00:10:11.083 --> 00:10:13.548
at a big New York law firm

00:10:13.548 --> 00:10:15.408
but even though it had been my dream,

00:10:15.408 --> 00:10:18.792
I didn't like it once I got there.

00:10:18.792 --> 00:10:21.966
For the first time, I was
feeling financially secure

00:10:21.966 --> 00:10:23.951
but I knew I wasn't passionate enough

00:10:23.951 --> 00:10:26.050
about what I was doing,

00:10:26.050 --> 00:10:28.250
and because I didn't love it,

00:10:28.250 --> 00:10:30.365
I would never be fulfilled from it,

00:10:30.365 --> 00:10:32.775
or be really good at it.

00:10:32.775 --> 00:10:33.994
Someone who loved it more

00:10:33.994 --> 00:10:36.975
would have greater enthusiasm and focus.

00:10:36.975 --> 00:10:38.715
For some, it was fun.

00:10:38.715 --> 00:10:42.806
For me, it was always going
to be a horrible chore.

00:10:42.806 --> 00:10:45.202
After five years in a law firm,

00:10:45.202 --> 00:10:48.517
I decided I wanted to
try something different.

00:10:48.517 --> 00:10:52.317
I came home and told my wife
I was quitting and she cried

00:10:52.317 --> 00:10:54.306
(audience laughing)

00:10:54.306 --> 00:10:55.623
and not out of happiness.

00:10:55.623 --> 00:10:57.318
(audience laughing)

00:10:57.318 --> 00:10:59.094
Anyway it worked out.

00:10:59.094 --> 00:11:01.739
I got a new job at a small Wall Street

00:11:01.739 --> 00:11:04.477
having been rejected at every other one.

00:11:04.477 --> 00:11:06.911
We got put out by a larger firm,

00:11:06.911 --> 00:11:10.218
and I ended up remaining at
that large firm Goldman Sachs.

00:11:10.218 --> 00:11:11.862
Hear the question, why do
you think Goldman Sachs

00:11:11.862 --> 00:11:13.347
is better than everybody else?

00:11:13.347 --> 00:11:14.335
- I do what I assume.

00:11:14.335 --> 00:11:16.023
I assume you had the best people.

00:11:16.023 --> 00:11:19.490
- 'Cause we recruit and
hire the best people,

00:11:19.490 --> 00:11:23.867
and more importantly
because we retain them,

00:11:23.867 --> 00:11:25.890
and the reason why we retain them,

00:11:25.890 --> 00:11:28.959
and the reason why we
get the best people is,

00:11:28.959 --> 00:11:30.990
and I believe this.

00:11:30.990 --> 00:11:32.010
- [Interviewer] I want you to say it.

00:11:32.010 --> 00:11:33.749
- You got to let me say this.

00:11:33.749 --> 00:11:37.916
Because we get people who
are really interested in

00:11:39.665 --> 00:11:40.915
doing something

00:11:42.401 --> 00:11:44.318
that they think is good

00:11:45.943 --> 00:11:48.796
for the public, for the world they're in.

00:11:48.796 --> 00:11:51.144
You mentioned earlier my predecessors

00:11:51.144 --> 00:11:52.466
who became secretaries of treasury.

00:11:52.466 --> 00:11:54.763
I could take you back and give you 100.

00:11:54.763 --> 00:11:56.247
The people we get,

00:11:56.247 --> 00:11:58.706
a kind of person at Goldman Sachs,

00:11:58.706 --> 00:12:02.225
who really wants to be
an influential person,

00:12:02.225 --> 00:12:05.809
who wants to do something
that's important,

00:12:05.809 --> 00:12:06.642
who feels that the job--

00:12:06.642 --> 00:12:07.475
- [Interviewer] Who
would make a lot of money

00:12:07.475 --> 00:12:09.689
and then go out and do good?

00:12:09.689 --> 00:12:11.731
- The people that we have

00:12:11.731 --> 00:12:14.240
would like to do well for themselves also

00:12:14.240 --> 00:12:18.576
but most of them, at the
height of their careers,

00:12:18.576 --> 00:12:20.184
go into public service.

00:12:20.184 --> 00:12:21.634
I mean the record is repeated

00:12:21.634 --> 00:12:24.398
not just in the US but
all around the world,

00:12:24.398 --> 00:12:25.839
not just at the top of the firm

00:12:25.839 --> 00:12:26.956
but in the middle of the firm.

00:12:26.956 --> 00:12:28.322
- So you're saying the
secret to Goldman Sachs

00:12:28.322 --> 00:12:30.267
is that they hire the right people?

00:12:30.267 --> 00:12:32.224
- Right, we hire the right people,

00:12:32.224 --> 00:12:33.710
and retain the right people,

00:12:33.710 --> 00:12:36.114
and at certain times,
lose the right people

00:12:36.114 --> 00:12:38.060
and make room for other
right people to come in.

00:12:38.060 --> 00:12:39.349
- Thank you so much for watching.

00:12:39.349 --> 00:12:42.172
I made this video because
Martel Thomas asked me to.

00:12:42.172 --> 00:12:43.482
So if there's a famous entrepreneur

00:12:43.482 --> 00:12:45.424
that you want me to profile next,

00:12:45.424 --> 00:12:46.663
leave it in the comments below,

00:12:46.663 --> 00:12:47.779
and I'll see what I can do.

00:12:47.779 --> 00:12:51.512
I also love to know which
of the top 10 rules today

00:12:51.512 --> 00:12:53.658
hit you the hardest,
meant the most to you.

00:12:53.658 --> 00:12:54.818
Leave it in the comments below.

00:12:54.818 --> 00:12:55.685
Join the discussion.

00:12:55.685 --> 00:12:56.931
And my personal goal in this channel

00:12:56.931 --> 00:12:58.297
is to hit one million subscribers

00:12:58.297 --> 00:13:00.525
so anything you can think
of to help share the videos,

00:13:00.525 --> 00:13:02.627
spread the message, tweet it @ThePlaylist,

00:13:02.627 --> 00:13:04.443
I'd really, really appreciate it.

00:13:04.443 --> 00:13:05.932
Thank you, guys, so much for watching.

00:13:05.932 --> 00:13:08.841
Continue to believe,
and I'll see you soon.

00:13:08.841 --> 00:13:09.674
(air whooshing)

00:13:09.674 --> 00:13:11.262
(heavy object thuds)

